EXAMPLE 1  (p.5):  A1 = F_2[x1,x2]/<t1,t2>
==============================================================================
  t1 = x^5+x^4+1 : irreducible factors of degrees [2, 3]
  t2 = x^4+x^2+x : irreducible factors of degrees [1, 3]
  (paper p.5 writes t2 = x2(x2^2+x2+1); that product is x^3+x^2+x,
   not x^4+x^2+x.  Correct: t2 = x2(x2^3+x2+1).  Minor slip.)
  prod deg t_i = 5 * 4 = 20
  number of classes |S-hat| = sum over factor pairs of gcd(d1,d2) = 6
    (paper p.5 says six -- agrees)
  sum of |S(nu)| over classes = sum over factor pairs of d1*d2 = 20
    and prod deg t_i = 20  ->  equal: True
  => dim_2 A1 = 20, but |S-hat| = 6.  Even Example 1 is
     not the split case: class sizes are 2,6,3,3,3,3, not all 1.

==============================================================================
COMPONENT-LEVEL CHECK: each C_S lives in A_S^n, not in F_q^n
==============================================================================
  component   dim_{A_S}  hull_{A_S} |S|  dim_q   hull_q
  Ex2 comp 1  4          0          2    8       0
  Ex2 comp 2  6          0          2    12      0
  Ex3 comp 1  10         2          2    20      4
  Ex3 comp 2  12         0          2    24      0

  Ex 2: sum of A_S-dimensions = 4+6 = 10  (the paper's printed value)
        weighted by |S|=2      = 20  (the true F_9-dimension)
        hull: 0+0 = 0 either way -- Ex 2's hull value is unaffected
  Ex 3: sum of A_S-dimensions = 10+12 = 22 (paper)
        weighted by |S|=2      = 44 (true F_25-dimension)
        hull: 2+0 = 2 (paper)  vs  4+0 = 4 (true F_25-dimension)

  This also confirms the diagnosis: the paper's own arithmetic is
  internally consistent, and reproduces exactly, when every dimension
  is read as an A_S-dimension.  The missing step is the factor |S| = 2.

==============================================================================
GENERAL STATEMENT (derived, not quoted)
==============================================================================
  A = F_q[X_1,..,X_l]/<t_1(X_1),..,t_l(X_l)> with the t_i squarefree.
  CRT (paper p.5):  A ~= (+)_S F_q(nu_1,..,nu_l), and each summand is a
  field of degree |S(nu)| over F_q.  Hence
      dim_q A = sum_S |S(nu)| = prod_i deg t_i.
  Writing a = sum_S a_S e_S therefore requires a_S in F_{q^|S(nu)|},
  NOT in F_q, unless every |S(nu)| = 1 (i.e. every t_i splits).

  Paper's own numbers, three rings:
    Example 1 (q=2):  |S-hat| = 6, dim_q A = 20
    Example 2 (q=9):  |S-hat| = 2, dim_q A = 4
    Example 3 (q=25): |S-hat| = 2, dim_q A = 4
    In every case dim_q A exceeds N, and they differ whenever some
    t_i has degree > 1 -- which holds in all three examples.

  Consequences (all arithmetic, no interpretation):
    Ex 2: claimed [14,10,4]_9.  dim_F9 C = 20 > 14 = nN.
    Ex 3: claimed [26,22,4]_25. dim_F25 C = 44 > 26 = nN.
    No injective F_9-linear (resp. F_25-linear) map from such a module
    into F_q^{nN} exists, so psi in eq.(10) is not the claimed bijection.

  Repair R1 (add 'every t_i splits'): consistent, but all three of the
    paper's rings fail it, so every example and Table 1 would be void.
  Repair R2 (psi : A -> F_q^{dim_q A} via F_q-bases): length becomes
    n*dim_q A (28 and 52), and psi is only F_q-linear, so Lemma 9
    (psi(hull) = hull(psi(C))) no longer follows from an isometry.
